## Why This Is Asked
Tests hardware offload, OS UDP paths, and QoS interplay in a multi-region fabric, focusing on telemetry reliability under bursts with no app changes.

## Key Concepts
- NIC RSS hashing and RX queues
- UDP tail drops and sockstat counters
- VXLAN/EVPN MTU and fragmentation
- Per-tenant rate limiting and edge QoS

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple UDP burst tester (Node.js)
const dgram = require('dgram');
const client = dgram.createSocket('udp4');
const TARGET = {host: 'collector.example', port: 9000};
let sent = 0, burst = 1000;
function sendNext(){
  if (sent >= burst) { client.close(); return; }
  client.send(Buffer.from('x'), 0, 1, TARGET.port, TARGET.host, () => {});
  sent++;
  if (sent % 10 === 0) setImmediate(sendNext);
  else setTimeout(sendNext, 0);
}
sendNext();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you differentiate RSS-induced drops from MTU fragmentation issues?
- Which measurements would you automate for a non-disruptive rollback if a mitigation fails?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to diagnose cross-region telemetry issues in a service mesh with TLS, tenancy boundaries, and UDP paths.

## Key Concepts
- Edge load balancers TLS session resumption; per-tenant sidecar resource usage; MTU discovery and path fragmentation;
- NAT64 state handling; cross-region tunnel dynamics; observability across L4-L7 boundaries.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: quick script outline to surface TLS ticket counts from edge LB logs
const fs = require('fs');
const logs = fs.readFileSync('/var/log/edge/tls.log','utf8');
const tickets = logs.match(/TLS_ticket:(\d+)/g) || [];
console.log('Tickets seen:', tickets.length);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you safely roll out TLS ticket lifetime changes across regions?
- Which metrics differentiate sidecar starvation from path MTU fragmentation in this scenario?
